+"""Phase-specific prompts for the coding agent's multi-phase workflow.
+
+Placeholders are filled with str.format(); literal braces must not appear
+in these templates except as placeholders.
+"""
+
+PLAN_AND_IMPLEMENT = """I have uploaded a code repository in the directory {git_tempdir}. Help solve the following problem.
+
+<problem_description>
+{problem_statement}
+</problem_description>
+
+Your task is to make changes to the files in the {git_tempdir} directory to address the <problem_description>. I have already taken care of the required dependencies.
+
+PHASE 1: PLAN AND IMPLEMENT.
+1. Read the statement and constraints carefully. Note the input format, output format, and exact limits (n, values, time limit if given).
+2. Write a short plan to `{git_tempdir}/notes.md` containing:
+   - Analysis of the problem and the key observation(s).
+   - An estimate of the intended time and memory complexity, and whether it fits the limits in Python (Python does roughly 10^7 simple operations per second; aim well under that).
+   - The simplest correct algorithm that fits the limits. Prefer simple over clever when both are correct.
+   - An edge-case list (e.g. n=1, all equal values, empty or minimal input, maximum values, worst-case structure, overflow-like sizes, deep recursion chains).
+3. Implement the solution in `{git_tempdir}/solution.py` with the editor tool.
+   - If the problem is a complete program, it must read standard input via `sys.stdin` and write to standard output.
+   - If the problem asks for a method of a `Solution` class, define `class Solution` with exactly the requested method name and signature, and begin the file with `from typing import *`.
+   - `solution.py` must be self-contained: no dependence on helper files, notes, or test data.
+4. Run `python3 -m py_compile {git_tempdir}/solution.py` to make sure it compiles.
+5. Run it on the sample inputs from the statement and compare with the expected outputs exactly.
+
+Do not stop until `solution.py` exists, compiles, and reproduces the samples. Use the tools as needed; do not just describe the solution.
+"""
+
+VERIFY_AND_REPAIR = """PHASE 2: VERIFY AND REPAIR (round {round_number} of {max_rounds}).
+
+Here is the automatic gate report for `{git_tempdir}/solution.py`. It contains raw output from checking the file exists, compiles, has the expected entry point, and runs on sample files. Read it yourself; it is not parsed:
+
+<gate_report>
+{gate_report}
+</gate_report>
+
+If the gate report shows any problem (missing file, compile error, crash, non-zero exit, wrong output on a sample), fix it first.
+
+Then verify the solution thoroughly, as follows:
+1. Write a brute-force reference solution for small inputs, in a separate file such as `{git_tempdir}/brute.py`. It should be obviously correct even if slow.
+2. Write a random small-input generator in `{git_tempdir}/gen.py` that takes a seed as its command-line argument (use `random.seed(int(sys.argv[1]))`) and prints one valid input to stdout. Include edge values in the random choices (minimum sizes, equal values, extremes of the value range).
+3. Run a stress test of at least 300 random small cases comparing `solution.py` with `brute.py`. You can use the `stress_test` tool, or a bash loop over seeds. For a `Solution` class method, call the method from a driver script and compare with the brute force's result. Print the first mismatching input and both outputs. Fix every mismatch in `solution.py` and re-run until zero mismatches.
+4. Run a maximum-size timing test: generate an input at the largest allowed size (and worst-case structure, e.g. deep chains or many duplicates), then run `time python3 solution.py < big.txt`. The run must finish well under 2 seconds. If it is slow, improve the algorithm (not just micro-optimise). Use `sys.stdin` for fast reading and avoid recursion depth issues (raise `sys.setrecursionlimit` and/or use an iterative approach).
+5. Re-run the samples and confirm the output format is exact (no extra spaces, debug prints, or missing newlines).
+
+Keep helper files (brute.py, gen.py, driver scripts) outside solution.py. Solution.py must not import them.
+
+When finished, end your reply with exactly one of these lines on its own:
+CHECKS: PASSED   (only if the gate is clean, the stress test had at least 300 cases with zero mismatches, and the timing test passed)
+CHECKS: FAILED   (otherwise)
+"""
+
+FINAL_REVIEW = """PHASE 3: FINAL REVIEW.
+
+Review `{git_tempdir}/solution.py` against this checklist and fix anything that fails. Re-run the samples after any change.
+- Fast IO: uses `sys.stdin` (e.g. `sys.stdin.read().split()`) and writes output once, not per-line `print` in huge loops, when there are many outputs.
+- Recursion: no deep recursion that could exceed Python's default limit. Either use an iterative DFS/BFS or raise the recursion limit together with `threading` stack size.
+- No debug output: no stray `print`, no logging, no input prompts, no leftover test code under the entry point.
+- Exact output format: matches the statement (separators, newlines, case, YES/NO spelling, floating-point precision if specified).
+- Correct entry point: for a `Solution` task, `class Solution` with the exact method name and signature from the problem, and `from typing import *` at the top. For a stdin program, the code runs on import-free execution and handles the full input.
+- Self-contained: `solution.py` imports only the standard library and does not depend on any helper file, notes, or test data.
+- Edge cases from your notes are handled.
+
+When done, reply with a short summary of the final state, and end with exactly one line: CHECKS: PASSED or CHECKS: FAILED.
+"""
